#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130410 :tutorial/content "Recall that the double Integral of the function $f(x,y) = 1$ over a domain $D\\subset \\mathbb R^2$ is simply the area of $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}\\ \\!(D) = \\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} 1 \\cdot \\textrm d A = \\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}  \\ \\textrm d A\n$$\n\nWe have a similar result for triple integrals:\n\n> *If $R$ is a region in three-dimensional space, then the volume of $R$ is given by*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\textrm{Volume}(R) = \\iiint\\limits_{R} \\textrm{d}V.\n> $$\n\nIn other words, the volume of $R$ equals the triple integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=1$ over $R.$ \n\nFor example, let's use triple integrals to find the volume of the rectangular prism shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8f9ab5db03f1cdf84ef484aca1ab89995237e7ef333c9b7c563d0080d317e981.png)\n\nNotice that our solid can be written in set-builder notation as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 3 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}dx\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dy\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dz \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We get the same value using the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\text{length}\\cdot \\text{width}\\cdot \\text{height} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "8b8f4748-190d-5fda-9a83-139e6a994d94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4911 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362409 :tutorial/content "When a solid is described in words, we first identify its bounding surfaces and then decide how to describe the entire region with inequalities.\n\nA useful strategy is:\n\n- identify the variable that lies between a lower surface and an upper surface,\n\n- determine the projection of the solid onto a coordinate plane,\n\n- write bounds for the projected region,\n\n- use those bounds to set up a triple integral with integrand equal to ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg).\n\nIn many volume problems, the solid lies between a bottom surface\n![](images/8e/8eeea9a9caa745d87342df718b249603d257e35820bea2df1f1d647d61877c0c.svg)\nand a top surface\n![](images/21/211a455fb9c563450b5b5225f0d3615467c89870e467920ad73beacb802375b8.svg)\n\nIn that case, we describe the solid by\n![](images/eb/eb36f1bb66ca8c06c73de972395684c368cc3cdba4d05b9300dee9f852c3174b.svg)\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the projection of the solid onto the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nSo, to find the volume, we use\n![](images/c6/c6fd71393350dac5cd3d1067227863cab1e92534812ea083231408ce660143bf.svg)\n\nSo, the verbal description tells us two things:\n\n- which surface is below and which is above,\n\n- which points ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) belong to the base region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg).\n\nBefore integrating, it is always a good idea to check that the upper surface is actually above the lower surface on the entire projected region." :tutorial/id #uuid "8f80f606-7a23-56be-8c78-48b9eeaa0fd7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15980 :tutorial/title "Interpreting a Verbal Description of a Solid"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362410 :tutorial/content "Consider the volume of the solid region enclosed by the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane, the plane ![](images/af/af57fba1cdb37044c8a4f922ba0ce95fcae5347960e050ac391af840198aca28.svg) and the planes ![](images/89/89c97ad93c125fc2b6f61d44fe9a90845fd289329d79d506c857f970d7586540.svg) ![](images/48/480183a1321e5b4fdff9500a551818f6fb0ab2713c0fdd8fa9df88a640394af9.svg) ![](images/8d/8de04eef03bf4f5fd455bcd39f1d747bc5e38650a1f8c6dd5249166cf5fc022d.svg) and ![](images/ca/ca7dadeea1df352335554e1c48b6398167035636994354744737e4c75a1b0134.svg)\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region.\n\nFirst, the phrase **enclosed by the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane** tells us that the bottom of the solid is\n![](images/ef/efcfb7a56c85727ded8a9d5c9bd75fd79ac0bc20ba202c2da6aaca3c4e3ba503.svg)\n\nThe plane\n![](images/21/211cfe0529803c8450173dbcaa60c66f276389b5944047bc390738703f657309.svg)\nis the top of the solid.\n\nAlso, the planes\n![](images/a3/a3fe77f111727e68d20276851f67f7fc662eaea5df49eaf96695a9234f6304bf.svg)\ndescribe the projection of the solid onto the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane. So the base region is the rectangle\n![](images/59/5925cb0d118a2a3457f4683c7b38bfa59bc5e8bbc106af32f0364a0207f9af25.svg)\n\nTherefore, for each point ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) in this rectangle, the variable ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg) runs from the bottom surface to the top surface:\n![](images/b1/b1af88023edd0b854bb6b598521693ebf4e941d8a5567e6801801f1deeacb902.svg)\n\nSo the volume is\n![](images/bd/bd9acbbd0fb9472aafdc28fe38d7cf6a103c123ff999116cefca75f8dbf624a8.svg)\n\nNow we evaluate:\n\n![](images/fa/fa67106261e7d847a0be115b74ca3ebaa5d5aff7ceca30b0b94b950fb1b741cd.svg)\n\nTherefore, the volume of the solid is ![](images/85/85ab4d14e4ae710116ac84de4b03b3cf35723ab969b7abfba97889c497974147.svg)\n\nIn problems like this, the planes involving only ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) usually describe the base region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane, while the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane and the surface or plane involving ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg) describe the lower and upper bounds for ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg)." :tutorial/id #uuid "ca3b6881-db7f-536a-b48b-77adde0a1b19" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15981 :tutorial/title "Setting Up Volume from a Verbal Description"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362412 :tutorial/content "Sometimes a solid is enclosed between two surfaces. In these cases, we can compute its volume using a double integral over the projection region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nSuppose a solid is enclosed between the paraboloids\n![](images/7b/7b63c2c4326ca1b7c5001ce0fbed7a7f0e4c5f54a8b4b26eca446f2b2b65dfa5.svg)\n\nTo compute its volume, we use\n![](images/83/83fed741dc600a6aa368543b2522f90191579e318c653b03a6924fc9ffa1868d.svg)\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the projection region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nFirst, we find where the two surfaces intersect. This gives the boundary of the projection region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane:\n![](images/3d/3d710d141ba98ae84f8c82b8ba0233781fa3627b639857710981c6c657fac203.svg)\n\nSo, the projection region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the disk\n![](images/d5/d5eef3331fa2e621c6a523f562fc759540299b92a24dfb1502b0d73c11377157.svg)\n\nThe height of the solid is the difference between the upper surface (![](images/cd/cdac8cf874dd0689e35a1c8d1a790bd5279d33ca80f0fd6fb4a2034e4a8b35f4.svg)) and the lower surface (![](images/69/6963e5988546516a47de0a33528be8c2c1b4626d97d9730e8bb950aa9100fe92.svg)):\n![](images/50/50c6958184049a24a56265775b5500b62bac9face27b2893824ed7d1b58d70ec.svg)\n\nIf we write ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) using ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) as the outer variable, then\n![](images/67/671080e239a3f478bcd6b47f0b6b31bdd3bef18de65edc92ac956d4a5cf07683.svg)\n\nThe volume is given by the iterated integral:\n![](images/18/180f43f3029d64ca0fe7971d108167a900d05ddbaa9aed0e017e3ef85cf2c279.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "efa958a5-e1ae-5402-b9a3-980b67367758" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15982 :tutorial/title "Using a Projection Region to Set Up a Double Integral"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362411 :tutorial/content "Alternatively, we can conceptualize the volume of a solid using a **triple integral**. \n\nRevisiting the previous example enclosed between the paraboloids, our solid can be formally written as a 3D region:\n![](images/17/17cee1ab33ee8dcd73e0c3a0151c563a6c64ca1c898383dbbca4d8ac7f1e0ff5.svg)\n\nThis means that for each point ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) in the projection region ![](images/87/87e7e0743ecd6609730f4b9a9b3681b1f360ca86bcb209e03561a197b18782f3.svg) the variable ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg) runs from the lower surface \n![](images/6a/6a37b724b57c8f5650e5970d1f90da49fe6a482582c7776aa567d41c2f6c406b.svg)\nup to the upper surface \n![](images/58/585d8d094c7eea9f1d7b242bebd015c27b39692e2cddbbaa76c0a87ce65b3ea5.svg)\n\nThe volume of ![](images/08/085f4d77675f3cb7323d94feaa9eaa38f2fecb26c0f8483895f9ddf36b8d319d.svg) is given by evaluating a triple integral of ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) over the region. By computing the innermost integral with respect to ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg), this calculation logically reduces to the double integral formula:\n![](images/8e/8e024d3e6c21346148c18139918dc5780683039bb34749489e188740ec3fa836.svg)\n\nIf we write ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) using ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) as the outer variable, then\n![](images/d4/d4cdea1370f4184ff8a5387dd95ed1cedd1b81d6027c32eea9240549d0dff0a4.svg)\nso\n![](images/10/109e5d163609178b9c69ae87bd236d3e0a8830157556082f92f2933c8b6d215b.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "ea755805-d530-504f-965f-afb35bf9a5b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15983 :tutorial/title "Connecting Triple Integrals and Double Integrals for Volume"}]}
